## Direct runtime offset reset

- Added the shared nullable-key/nullable-offset `ConsumerOffsetTable` carrier,
  public `ClientRuntime::reset_offset` and Push owner adapter. Inbound reset
  and the owner's timestamp-reset helper now use this runtime entrypoint.
- Source review found the old helper reused first-pass map values and queue
  membership after its drain wait and returned a sibling queue's persistence
  error. It now reads the current shared table and current queue table for the
  second pass, logs/continues per-queue failures, and reads the current store.
  A private finally guard restores pause/scheduler state even on cancellation.
- The Java reset method shares synchronized(this) with lifecycle and
  producer/consumer registry operations. Added the private runtime monitor to
  reset, producer/consumer registration/removal and start; shutdown preserves
  Java's active-user precheck before monitor acquisition. Raw map and admin
  APIs continue to bypass that monitor like Java.
- Added fifteen actual Java runtime/rebalance cases: missing/Pull/null groups,
  empty/null topic/table/key/value branches, unassigned queues, successful and
  per-queue-failed persistence, live map update, newly inserted queue and
  orderly reset. The probe overrides resume to record the finally action
  without an unrelated rebalance. Java's ignored sleep-interruption path bounds
  the ordinary cases; the live cases use a clear latch and worker interrupt.
  Per-queue events are grouped across unordered ConcurrentHashMap iteration;
  status, ordering within each queue and data/ownership outcomes are preserved.
- The initial existing-race run exposed a test harness discrepancy: manual
  Started-state installation omitted the raw offset-store field that real
