## Offset-store callback handoff refinement

- Java rereads `getOffsetStore()` at update, persist and remove boundaries.
  Added an actual Java store-handoff case that changes the field in update
  and persist callbacks: the first store retains 7, the second persists 60,
  and the third removes its 70. Rust now rereads the field at each boundary.
  Orderly second-persist exceptions preserve the registered queue rather than
  silently treating them as an unsuccessful unlock result.
- Moved custom remove_offset invocation outside the process-queue map write
  lock; a private test hook inspects that map from the callback to detect a
  reentrancy deadlock. Hooks remain cfg(test), not public client seams/settings.
- Strengthened the Java live-map/new-queue probe to observe the actual drain
  sleep stack frame after the clear latch before mutation/interrupt, avoiding
  reliance on ConcurrentHashMap iterator prefetch. Regenerated fixture SHA-256:
  `ed123fb25e329d0166329dea123add868ac9846ffc251e2469970493691b9665`.
- After generation completed, Consumer reset filter passed 11 tests, including
  all fifteen map/queue cases, callback/pull fences, orderly removal, cancelled
  caller resume, and the new three-store handoff/lock regression.
