## Runtime monitor cancellation ownership

- Extended the existing scripted Broker unregister-cancellation test with a
  reset started after caller cancellation but before the Broker reply. It
  failed: a caller-owned monitor guard released admission while the actor's
  unregister command was still running. The reset overtook that operation.
- Added private monitored-command envelopes and one shared owned guard. The
  caller retains it through post-processing; the actor retains it through
  completion after caller cancellation. Abandoned registration replies carry
  it into identity cleanup, including waits for the key's mutation gate.
  No public knob, executor, worker or test seam was added.
- The scripted cancellation case now passes. A focused internal regression
  holds a raw key gate to keep abandoned-registration cleanup pending, proves
  reset cannot enter early, releases the key and proves both identity cleanup
  and reset complete. The test passes. Core lifecycle (5) and direct runtime
  operations (2), strict workspace Clippy, fmt and diff checks also pass.
- Committing this cancellation correction independently; final current-source
  broad gates must be rerun after this control-flow refinement.
- Monitor ownership correction committed as `6b5129f1`.

